+# Beyond XI — Ball possession labelling (More Than A Hack 2027, Phase 1)
+
+**Submission:** `Beyond_XI.csv` (199,319 rows, passes `validate_submission.py`)
+**Code:** this repository; one command regenerates the file byte for byte (see *Reproduction*).
+
+## 1. Approach in brief
+
+The pipeline labels each frame in three steps:
+
+1. **Dead ball (rules).** A frame is `D` when the ball is beyond a line, when the ball has been
+   untracked for 2 s or more, when players collectively walk (median speed below 1 m/s) for 2 s or
+   more, inside a Smart Tagging set-piece window until the restart kick, and before each half's
+   kick-off.
+2. **Who has the ball (learned).** A gradient-boosted tree classifier (scikit-learn
+   `HistGradientBoostingClassifier`) predicts P(home in possession) on every live frame from 34
+   home-minus-away features:
+   - *ball evidence*: each team's nearest-player distance; **touch** (a player within 1.5 m of the
+     tracked ball at any speed, at least 1 m nearer than any opponent); **control** (within 2 m of
+     a ball slower than 7 m/s, same margin); ball speed;
+   - *team structure and movement*, in each team's attacking direction (inferred per half without
+     labels): centroid advance, centroid forward velocity, width, length, mean speed, players
+     within 5 m of the ball;
+   - centred averages of these over ±0.25 s, ±1 s and ±2.5 s.
+3. **Possessions (Viterbi).** Inside each live stretch a two-state Viterbi decoder turns the
+   per-frame log-probabilities into a home/away sequence, paying a fixed cost for every change of
+   team. Possession changes only when the model is confidently on the other side for long
+   enough, and the change lands where the evidence turns.
+
+The model is trained only on public matches from other competitions (Section 3). No label of the
+challenge match is used for training, tuning or validation.
+
+**Why this design.** The ranking metric (spellF1) rewards correct possession boundaries, not
+per-frame accuracy, so we built a validation harness that measures spellF1 on matches with known
+possession and kept only changes that improved it consistently. We started from hand-written rules
+(nearest player, control duration, consecutive touches); the learned model with Viterbi decoding
+replaced them once it beat them on held-out matches (validation spellF1 0.578 → 0.642 on the
+original three matches).
+
+## 2. Assets used and dropped
+
+| Asset | Used? | How / why not |
+|---|---|---|
+| Tactical automatic tracking (ATD) | **Yes** | all features; the ball's absence is the main dead-ball cue |
+| Smart Tagging — set pieces | **Yes** | dead-ball windows before restarts; kick-off timing |
+| Smart Tagging — phases of play | Tried, dropped | build-up/progression tags agree with the possessing team ~88% of the time, but they are ~19 s windows spanning several possessions; with realistically aligned synthetic tags on the training matches they gave no gain (0.647 vs 0.642) |
+| Homography | No | only needed to project onto video |
+| Tactical and broadcast video | No | the video files were not part of our download, and our error analysis showed most remaining errors occur with the ball tracked |
+| Broadcast tracking | No | different timeline, no mapping to the tactical frames |
+
+## 3. Training and validation data
+
+All labelled data is from **other matches**, publicly available; nothing was hand-labelled by us.
+
+| Source | Matches | Possession truth | Used for |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Metrica Sports sample data (github.com/metrica-sports/sample-data) | 3 | derived from Metrica's human-annotated events | training + validation |
+| IDSSE / DFL open data (Bassek et al. 2025, *Scientific Data*, CC-BY 4.0, data owner Deutsche Fußball Liga) | 7 Bundesliga | the tracking's own per-frame `BallPossession` / `BallStatus` | training + validation |
+| SkillCorner open data | 20 A-League | per-frame team possession | tried as training data, dropped (Section 5) |
+
+**Controlled possession.** Both raw truths flip on every touch (DFL: 37% of spells under 2 s). Following
+CHALLENGE.md ("a simple touch without control" is not a change of possession), a new team's spell
+counts only if it shows control: 2 s of live play, or, for the event-based Metrica truth, a
+completed pass, carry, shot or set piece. Otherwise it merges back into the previous possession.
+The resulting possession lengths match the shape of the public 5-minute answer key (median 6.9–7.3 s
+vs 7.6 s; under 2 s: 5–13% vs 12%). The public answer key was used only for this shape check and for
+format/alignment checks, never to choose between versions.
+
+**Making the public data look like the challenge tracking.** Training and validation tracking is
+degraded so that label-free statistics match the challenge match: smoothly drifting player error and
+dropouts; a drifting ball offset (~0.8 m) with small jitter; and, for the always-tracked DFL ball,
+ball loss on dead stretches and in short live gaps calibrated on Metrica's real gaps. We check
+ball-tracked share, ball-to-player distances, touch and control rates and stoppage behaviour against
+the real match (`scripts/compare_harness_realism.py`).
+
+**Validation protocol.** Leave-one-game-out over the 10 matches: each match is scored by a model trained
+on the other nine, with three degradation seeds, using the organisers' own metric code. A change was
+kept only if bootstrap intervals did not overlap or it improved nearly every held-out match.
+
+## 4. Possession rules implemented
+
+- **Loose and contested balls** stay with the last team in possession. Evidence only counts for a team
+  whose player is at least 1 m nearer the ball than any opponent; in duels and tight marking no team gets
+  evidence.
+- **A touch is not possession.** One touch or deflection rarely outweighs the decoder's switch cost; a
+  sustained run of evidence (control, consecutive touches, team movement) does.
+- **Restarts.** Dead ball lasts until the restart kick; after a dead stretch either team may take
+  possession at no extra cost, decided by the evidence that follows.
+- **Dead ball is never possession.** Before each half's kick-off is always `D`.
+- **Abstention.** We never write `X` on graded frames; ungraded frames are `X`.
+
+## 5. Results and what we learned
+
+| | Validation spellF1 (10 matches, leave-one-game-out) |
+|---|---|
+| Official baseline (nearest player, ±2 s vote) | 0.312 |
+| Our rule-based decoder (iteration 008, on 3 matches) | 0.578 |
+| Learned model + Viterbi, trained on Metrica only | 0.618 |
+| **Submitted: learned model + Viterbi, Metrica + DFL** | **0.629** |
+
+On the public 5-minute window (sanity only, 17 possessions) the submitted pipeline scores 0.497
+against the baseline's 0.326.
+
+Experiments that did not improve validation, kept as patches in `experiments/patches/`: trimming
+dead-ball gaps at restarts; keeping open-play ball gaps live; hand-weighted Viterbi scores; extra shape
+features (convex hull, defensive line, press height); Smart Tagging phase features; extra noisy copies of
+the training matches; SkillCorner broadcast tracking as training data (0.595 — its extrapolated
+broadcast positions look unlike a tactical camera); evidence-dependent switch costs from a learned switch
+model (0.636, within noise); boundary-weighted training; shorter feature windows. The full log with every
+number is in `ITERATIONS.md`.
+
+**Where the headroom is.** Replacing our dead-ball labels with the truth on the validation matches lifts
+spellF1 only to 0.666; replacing our team labels lifts it to 0.930. Of true open-play turnovers, 46% have no
+predicted switch within 5 s: they are short opponent possessions (won and lost within a few seconds) that
+the noisy ball evidence does not reveal. Long wrong-team stretches are rare (2% of live frames) and false
+switches are 13% of predicted switches.
+
+## 6. Limitations
+
+- **Short possessions are under-detected** (validation: 6,976 predicted vs 8,958 true possessions). We
+  attacked this through the decoder, the training emphasis and the features; none moved it beyond noise.
+  Better ball information (e.g. ball detection from video) is the likely route.
+- **Dead ball is over-predicted.** 47% of the challenge match is labelled `D`; an indirect estimate puts
+  the true share near 34%. On validation, 4.3% of frames are called dead while live, mostly long ball gaps
+  during live play.
+- **Validation truth is an approximation** of the organisers' corrected-tracking labels, built with our
+  controlled-possession rule.
+- **Small data.** Ten labelled matches from two providers; the degradation of their tracking is simulated,
+  though checked against the real match.
+
+## 7. Reproduction
+
+```bash
+uv venv --python 3.11 .venv
+uv pip install --python .venv -r requirements-mtah.txt
+# challenge bundle unpacked into data/tactical/  (see data/README.md)
+bash scripts/download_public_data.sh                     # Metrica + DFL open data, ~2.9 GB
+.venv/bin/python run.py --output Beyond_XI.csv          # trains, labels, validates
+.venv/bin/python run_validation.py                       # optional: leave-one-game-out validation
+```
+
+Deterministic: the same inputs give the same bytes (fixed seeds, single model).
+
+## 8. Tools and AI assistance
+
+Python 3.11, numpy, pandas, scikit-learn, kloppy. The code was developed with the help of Claude Code
+(Anthropic), an AI coding assistant. In line with the competition terms, its analysis of the challenge
+match used aggregate statistics (counts, rates, scores) computed locally, and project permission rules
+block it from reading the challenge data files.
